# yardr.epic: the flow of rig yardr's epics (yardr rig flow yardr --type epic
# yardr.epic). An epic waits in open while its children are open (each child
# blocks it); once the last closes, yardr-integrators rebases its branch onto
# main and gates it, Codex reviews the whole diff, and the epic lands on main
# locally, where the refinery fast-forwards main and advances it straight
# to merged (the bare edge, which no outcome offers), or, experimentally,
# through a pull request (approved -> in-pr -> land): only pr-epic reports
# done in approved, and without YARDR_PR=1 in its cmd it holds the epic
# instead, as pr-merge does in land. A rebase onto main
# that conflicts, in open or in approved, goes to the mergers; a resolved
# epic goes back to open, where it is integrated (and reviewed) again. See
# docs/pipeline.md#epics.
backlog -> open
open -> review on done | open on failed | merge on conflict | backlog
review -> approved on done | open on changes | decide on question
approved -> in-pr on done | open on failed | merge on conflict | merged
in-pr -> land on done | open on changes | decide on question
land -> merged on done | open on failed
merge -> open on done | open on rework | decide on question
decide -> open | backlog
merged terminal
